<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · disaster</p><h1 class="enc-title">Great Chinese Famine</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1959">1959</a>–<a href="/years/1961">1961</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Three years of great famine</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Great Chinese Famine was a famine in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> between 1959 and 1961, though some scholars include 1958 or 1962. Estimates of deaths from starvation range from 15 to 46 million, with newer estimates around 30 to 46 million. Its main causes were the policies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-leap-forward">Great Leap Forward</a> and the people's communes launched by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a>: high grain confiscation for export, poor farming techniques, the Eliminate Sparrows campaign, over-reporting of harvests and the shift of farmers to iron and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> production. Anhui, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guizhou">Guizhou</a> were among the worst hit. In 1962 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-shaoqi">Liu Shaoqi</a> attributed 70% of the famine to man-made errors.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Scholars disagree over the Holodomor as genocide, and Amartya Sen blamed the <b>Great Chinese Famine</b> on a lack of democracy.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/marxism-leninism">Marxism–Leninism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li></ul></section>
